WEBVTT

00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:02.789
What is the MAP method and how does it work?

00:00:03.079 --> 00:00:07.449
The MAP method is a powerful and widely used function in

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:11.079
JavaScript that operates on arrays.

00:00:11.239 --> 00:00:16.120
It is designed to create a new array by applying a given function

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.840
to each element of the original array.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.020
This method does not modify the original array,

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.239
but instead returns a new array containing the results of the function

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:28.639
applied to each element.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.830
Here's an example of using the map method on an array of numbers.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.720
To create a new array where each number is doubled,

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:38.470
we are using the map method.

00:00:38.909 --> 00:00:43.669
The map method accepts a callback function where the function is called

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:46.459
on every single element in the array.

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:47.689
In this case,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.340
each number in the array will be multiplied by 2.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.189
The result will be a new array of the numbers 2468,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:55.669
10.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.419
The callback function can accept up to 3 arguments.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.269
The first argument is the current element being processed.

00:01:05.879 --> 00:01:10.639
The second argument is the index of the current element being

00:01:10.639 --> 00:01:11.430
processed.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.529
The 3rd argument is the array,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.879
where map is being called on.

00:01:17.709 --> 00:01:22.709
Understanding and effectively using the map method can significantly

00:01:22.709 --> 00:01:26.580
improve your ability to work with arrays in JavaScript.

00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:28.400
In future lecture videos,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:33.430
we'll dive deeper into more advanced uses of map and explore how it

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:38.220
can be a powerful tool for building dynamic and efficient

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:38.790
programs.